  "theorem_name": "QuadraticResidueGaussZeroConvolutionCounterfamily",
  "declared_proposition": "For every prime p congruent to 3 modulo 4, let w_p be the indicator of the nonzero quadratic residues in Z/pZ. Its mass is W=(p-1)/2, but (w_p*w_p)(0)=0. For k nonzero, w_hat_p(k)=(-1+chi(k)tau_p)/2, where |tau_p|=sqrt(p) and tau_p^2=-p. Hence every nonprincipal magnitude is sqrt(p+1)/2 and max|w_hat_p(k)|/W=sqrt(p+1)/(p-1), which tends to zero. The signed nonprincipal aggregate at zero is exactly -W^2/p and cancels the principal term. Thus modewise relative Fourier decay does not imply pointwise positivity.",
  "mathematical_argument": "Because chi(-1)=-1, the negative of a nonzero quadratic residue is a nonresidue, proving the zero convolution. Writing the residue indicator as (1+chi)/2 away from zero gives the Gauss-sum formula. For p=3 mod 4 the quadratic Gauss sum is purely imaginary with square -p, so both signs have magnitude sqrt(p+1)/2. Fourier inversion at zero and the already proved zero convolution force the exact negative aggregate.",
